<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>

          <!--
          Test fixtures must not dilute the number. The endpoint fixtures in
          MintPlayer.AspNetCore.Endpoints.Generator.Tests exist purely to be fed to a
          generator driver; instrumenting them would add coverable lines that are not
          shipped code.
          -->
          <IncludeTestAssembly>false</IncludeTestAssembly>

          <!--
          The ONLY assembly exclusion, and it is deliberate.

          MintPlayer.AspNetCore.Endpoints.TestApp is IsPackable=false sample code. The
          end-to-end tests need a ProjectReference to it for WebApplicationFactory<Program>,
          which copies its DLL+PDB into the test output and makes it instrumentable. Sample
          code in the denominator is how you get a coverage number that means nothing: the
          sibling repo measured a project reporting 46.9% where 48% of the denominator was
          unreachable generator code, and the honest figure was 88.5%.

          Do NOT grow this list to hide uncovered shipped code. Everything else that is
          instrumented here is published to NuGet and belongs in the number.
          -->
          <Exclude>[MintPlayer.AspNetCore.Endpoints.TestApp]*</Exclude>

          <!--
          Pinned to false, not left to the default.

          SourceLink is ACTIVE in this repo: all 16 projects emit obj/**/*.sourcelink.json
          via the SDK-bundled SourceLink, triggered by the <RepositoryUrl>/<RepositoryType>
          metadata in every packable csproj (there is no Microsoft.SourceLink.* package
          reference anywhere, which is why it is easy to miss).

          UseSourceLink=true rewrites `filename` to raw.githubusercontent.com URLs. The
          coverage server suffix-matches report paths against `git ls-files` and requires a
          UNIQUE match; a URL tail makes every duplicated basename ambiguous, and ambiguous
          paths are dropped SILENTLY and excluded from the totals. This repo has five
          duplicated basenames (Image.cs, ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs, StringExtensions.cs,
          Url.cs, XmlSerializerOutputFormatter.cs), so that would lose real data with no error.
          -->
          <UseSourceLink>false</UseSourceLink>

          <!--
          Pinned to false so the coupling is visible rather than discovered.

          Correct TODAY only because ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true lives on the
          `dotnet pack` (with no-build) command in publish-release.yml, where it never reaches the
          compiler (the PDBs were already written by the Build step without it). Verified by
          rebuilding one project with the flag: its document map collapsed from
          C:\Repos\MintPlayer.AspNetCore.Tools\* to /_/*.

          If that property ever moves to a Directory.Build.props or onto the Build step,
          every path coverlet sees becomes /_/... and this MUST flip to true.
          -->
          <DeterministicReport>false</DeterministicReport>

          <!--
          Measured in the sibling repo as a 0.13pp change that also drops files from the
          server's file tree entirely. Not worth it.
          -->
          <SkipAutoProps>false</SkipAutoProps>

          <!--
          MEASURED, not copied. See the long note below for why the sibling repo's pattern
          did not transfer.

          The four libraries consuming MintPlayer.SourceGenerators report 8 generated
          documents under obj/. They do NOT exist on disk and are NOT git-tracked: they are
          virtual PDB paths for source the generator only ever held in memory. The coverage
          server therefore cannot resolve them against `git ls-files` and drops them
          silently, so leaving them in makes the local denominator disagree with the
          server's. Excluding them here makes the two agree.
          -->
          <ExcludeByFile>**/obj/**</ExcludeByFile>

          <!--
          ExcludeByFile is deliberately NOT SET YET. It must be decided by measurement
          against a real report, not copied from the sibling repo, because the situation
          here is different:

          The sibling's ExcludeByFile=**/*.g.cs removed 6 real files / 738 coverable lines
          of on-disk generated code. In THIS repo the external MintPlayer.SourceGenerators
          (10.13.0 — LoggerProviders, MustChangePassword, NoSniff, SitemapXml) writes
          NOTHING to disk. Verified: the only 16 *.g.cs files under obj/ are the SDK's own
          *.GlobalUsings.g.cs, and the obj/Release/net10.0/MintPlayer.SourceGenerators/.../
          ClassNames.g.cs paths that appear in build warnings DO NOT EXIST as files.
          EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles is set nowhere.

          Those synthetic paths still appear in the PDB and will show up in the report.
          Whether a coverlet file-glob can match a path with no file behind it is behaviour
          to be TESTED once a real report exists — not assumed. See PLAN M10.

          ExcludeByAttribute is also NOT set. The usual snippet is actively harmful; the
          sibling measured it: Obsolete -125 lines (Roslyn puts a synthetic [Obsolete] on
          `readonly ref struct`), CompilerGeneratedAttribute -194 lines (every async state
          machine body), GeneratedCodeAttribute 0.

          No <Include> allowlist either: instrumentation is already scoped by "has a PDB in
          the test output", and an allowlist is just a second place to forget a new package.
          -->

        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
